package handlers

// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// RATE-LIMIT BUCKET KEYS FOR THE TWO LOGIN ENDPOINTS.
//
// `rate_limits` is keyed on one column, `ClearAttempts` is
// `DELETE FROM rate_limits WHERE ip = ?`, and a SUCCESSFUL login calls it. So
// THE KEY IS THE ISOLATION. Two actions that spell the key the same way are one
// action as far as the limiter is concerned, and a success on either erases the
// failures of both.
//
// `/api/v1/auth/login` and `/api/v1/auth/login_token` are the same credential
// check reached two ways — cookie for the browser, bearer for the mobile app —
// and both used to key on a bare IP. The bypass that followed is not
// theoretical and does not need the lock to trip:
//
//	4 wrong passwords against any account  -> attempts = 4, no lock
//	1 correct login_token with an account
//	  the attacker legitimately holds      -> ClearAttempts, the ROW IS GONE
//	4 more wrong passwords                 -> attempts = 4 again
//	  ...
//
// Unbounded guessing at four per round, forever, and the fifth attempt is never
// reached — so the limiter never records a lock, never logs, and reports itself
// healthy the whole time. Pacing at four rather than five is what an attacker
// does because at five the shared lock refuses login_token as well; the shared
// bucket is what makes both halves of that work. Measured in a project built
// from this template, driving the real chi route table: 40 of 40 guesses
// reached bcrypt against a documented budget of 5. Namespaced, the same
// sequence gives 5 through and 35 refused.
//
// It needs one account of the attacker's own — a self-service signup on any app
// that also ran scaffold_registration, which is the pairing this tool set
// recommends.
//
// Namespacing also fixes the mirror nuisance: five bad browser logins from one
// office NAT would otherwise lock the MOBILE app for every user on that address.
//
// ─── why this is not two lines inside the handlers ───────────────────────────
//
// handlers/auth.go and handlers/mobile_auth.go are scaffold_auth output, and
// every scaffold writes with os.WriteFile — a re-run TRUNCATES them, along with
// both their _test.go files. A guard living inside the file it guards cannot
// survive the event it guards against. Keeping the keys here, with the test in
// handlers/auth_buckets_test.go, means the decision and its proof are in files
// whose whole content is this one concern: a re-run rewrites them with the same
// correct content, and a hand-edit that reverts a call site goes red. That test
// is the actual fail-loud mechanism — an unused function is not a Go compile
// error, so the file alone would not notice.
//
// Same reasoning that put clientIP in handlers/clientip.go. The customization
// left inside the two scaffolded handlers is deliberately one line each.
//
// ─── the spelling ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Bare action name plus the IP. The action names are the endpoints' own last
// path segments, so the key a row carries names the route that wrote it. Any
// later endpoint that counts failures — registration, password reset, an email
// resend — gets its own function here rather than reaching for a bare IP.
//
// The IP comes from clientIP (handlers/clientip.go), which has the trusted-proxy
// notion: behind a reverse proxy a key built from RemoteAddr would be ONE bucket
// for every caller on the planet.
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// loginBucket is POST /api/v1/auth/login — the cookie login.
func loginBucket(ip string) string { return "login:" + ip }

// loginTokenBucket is POST /api/v1/auth/login_token — the bearer login.
func loginTokenBucket(ip string) string { return "login_token:" + ip }

// registerBucket is POST /api/v1/auth/register.
//
// The banner above says any later endpoint that counts attempts gets its own
// function here rather than reaching for a bare IP; this is the first of them.
//
// Registration needed a bucket at all because it was the one unauthenticated
// write in the scaffold set with NO limit on it, and it answers 409 for an
// address that already has an account. Login goes to real trouble not to leak
// that — dummyHash exists so a missing account costs the same bcrypt time as a
// wrong password — and then registration published the same fact outright, one
// request per address, unmetered. The 409 is worth keeping (a signup form that
// cannot say "you already have an account" is a worse product), so the fix is
// to make asking expensive rather than to make the answer vague.
//
// Unlike the login buckets, EVERY attempt counts here, not only failures: the
// thing being limited is how fast one address can create accounts and probe
// addresses, and a successful signup is exactly that. Nothing clears this
// bucket; it decays on its own after the window, like every other bucket.
func registerBucket(ip string) string { return "register:" + ip }
